<Guide
  title="Offline Note-Taking App for Handwriting & Text | Myelin Notes"
  heading="Offline note-taking with handwriting and typed text"
  description="Write, sketch, and annotate PDFs without an internet connection. Myelin Notes stores your notes on your computer, with optional GitHub sync across Mac, Windows, and Linux."
>
  <h2>Keep a complete note on your computer</h2>
  <p>
    Myelin Notes is a local-first note-taking app: your workspace lives as files
    on your own disk. You can create and edit notes without a Myelin account or
    a cloud workspace. Handwriting, typed text, PDFs, images, and audio can live
    together on one canvas.
  </p>
  <p>
    That makes it useful for a lecture room with unreliable Wi-Fi, a train journey,
    or focused work with your connection turned off. Download and install the app
    first, and bring any reference files you need onto your computer before you go
    offline.
  </p>
  <figure>
    <Shot src="/library.png" alt="Myelin Notes library showing folders, recently opened canvas notes, and tags" eager />
    <figcaption>Organize your local workspace with folders and tags.</figcaption>
  </figure>

  <h2>What works offline?</h2>
  <table>
    <caption class="text-left font-medium">Offline features and optional online services</caption>
    <thead><tr><th scope="col">Activity</th><th scope="col">Connection needed?</th></tr></thead>
    <tbody>
      <tr><th scope="row">Write, type, and organize notes</th><td>No. Editing, folders, tags, and note links work locally.</td></tr>
      <tr><th scope="row">Annotate and export PDFs</th><td>No, once the PDF is on your computer.</td></tr>
      <tr><th scope="row">Search notes</th><td>No. Full-text and semantic search run locally.</td></tr>
      <tr><th scope="row">Record and transcribe audio</th><td>No. Audio transcription uses a model bundled with the app.</td></tr>
      <tr><th scope="row">Recognize handwriting</th><td>No connection on macOS. Handwriting OCR is not available on Windows or Linux.</td></tr>
      <tr><th scope="row">Sync through GitHub</th><td>Yes. Optional sync uses your GitHub account and repository.</td></tr>
      <tr><th scope="row">Collaborate live</th><td>Yes. Devices need a connection and access to the same repository.</td></tr>
    </tbody>
  </table>

  <h2>A workflow for handwritten and typed notes</h2>
  <ol>
    <li>Create a canvas note for a lecture, meeting, or research topic.</li>
    <li>Add a local PDF or image as reference material, then mark it up with the pen and highlighter.</li>
    <li>Add a page frame for a typed outline, checklist, or equations. Keep diagrams and handwritten working alongside it.</li>
    <li>Link related notes and add tags so you can find the material again.</li>
    <li>Export a PDF or image when you need a copy to share outside Myelin Notes.</li>
  </ol>
  <p>
    You can also record audio on the canvas and search its transcript locally.
    Transcription and semantic search run on your computer, so their speed depends
    on your hardware.
  </p>

  <h2>Sync across computers when you reconnect</h2>
  <p>
    If you work on more than one computer, optional GitHub sync connects your
    workspace to a repository and branch you control. Local editing does not
    require that connection. Sync does use GitHub’s service, so enabling it sends
    workspace data to your chosen repository.
  </p>
  <p>
    Live collaboration is a separate feature: devices with the same note open
    can edit together over an encrypted peer-to-peer connection. Both devices
    currently need access to the same GitHub repository. Single-note invitations
    with separate access roles are still coming soon.
  </p>

  <h2>Platforms, price, and your existing notes</h2>
  <p>
    Myelin Notes is available for Mac, Windows, and Linux, and is free for personal
    use. iPhone, iPad, and Android apps are in development. The source is public
    under PolyForm Strict 1.0.0; commercial use needs a separate license.
  </p>
  <p>
    Your workspace uses Myelin’s file format based on Yjs, rather than a folder of
    plain Markdown files. You can export notes to PDF, images, or JSON. If you are
    moving an existing notebook, follow the <a href="/guides/import-onenote/">OneNote import guide</a>.
    For pen input and platform-specific limitations, see
    <a href="/guides/linux-handwriting-notes/">handwriting notes on Linux</a>.
  </p>
</Guide>
